WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:03.630 --> 00:00:06.413
- [Narrator] The beauty of
Kauai just fills my soul.

00:00:09.390 --> 00:00:11.953
There are places of extreme beauty.

00:00:13.790 --> 00:00:16.810
And that for me is the Nā Pali Coast

00:00:16.810 --> 00:00:18.900
where there is a hidden gem.

00:00:18.900 --> 00:00:21.723
An old village site called Niihau Kauai.

00:00:26.770 --> 00:00:28.830
It is an absolutely ideal place

00:00:28.830 --> 00:00:31.190
to perpetuate Hawaiian culture.

00:00:32.751 --> 00:00:35.668
(reflective music)

00:00:40.826 --> 00:00:43.576
(Hawaiian music)

00:00:45.710 --> 00:00:48.260
I teach Hawaiian studies here in Kauai,

00:00:48.260 --> 00:00:50.384
and the children call me Cuomo Kauka.

00:00:50.384 --> 00:00:53.080
(Hawaiian music)

00:00:53.080 --> 00:00:56.233
I teach Hula, which is Hawaiian dance,

00:00:57.144 --> 00:01:00.963
Ōlelo Hawaii, which is Hawaiian language,

00:01:03.290 --> 00:01:06.549
and Kapa, which is Hawaiian barkcloth.

00:01:06.549 --> 00:01:08.300
- Oh my God, it's so beautiful.

00:01:08.300 --> 00:01:11.520
- [Narrator] In Hawaii, we
did not have loomed material.

00:01:11.520 --> 00:01:14.710
We made our clothing and our blankets

00:01:14.710 --> 00:01:16.453
from the bark of a tree.

00:01:20.010 --> 00:01:22.320
Kapa is made first by growing the plant

00:01:22.320 --> 00:01:23.492
and then harvesting it.

00:01:23.492 --> 00:01:25.640
Scraping off the outer bark.

00:01:25.640 --> 00:01:27.110
Removing the inner bark

00:01:27.110 --> 00:01:28.823
and pounding it out.

00:01:30.260 --> 00:01:32.760
You got to pound, and
pound, and pound and pound

00:01:32.760 --> 00:01:35.620
and then you need many, many
strips and you fold them

00:01:35.620 --> 00:01:38.013
together and pound them all out as one.

00:01:38.013 --> 00:01:40.750
(pounding sound)

00:01:40.750 --> 00:01:44.670
But you've got to have the
materials to teach Hawaiian arts.

00:01:44.670 --> 00:01:47.757
And those materials are
very much based on plants.

00:01:52.525 --> 00:01:55.525
(Helicopter sounds)

00:01:56.690 --> 00:02:00.660
We have to grow the Wauke
in order to make Kapa.

00:02:00.660 --> 00:02:05.143
That's why we have been return
to Niihau Kauai for 27 year.

00:02:08.750 --> 00:02:10.360
There are no roads in there.

00:02:10.360 --> 00:02:14.203
It is only accessible by boat or by air.

00:02:16.417 --> 00:02:19.440
(helicopter sound)

00:02:19.440 --> 00:02:22.310
You feel the sense of place when you land.

00:02:22.310 --> 00:02:24.480
When you walk along the little trail.

00:02:24.480 --> 00:02:26.162
And when you look out at the ocean

00:02:26.162 --> 00:02:29.891
and you see the turtles that
are resting on the beach.

00:02:29.891 --> 00:02:33.396
They have been on that
beach for centuries.

00:02:33.396 --> 00:02:36.223
We've only been on that
beach more recently.

00:02:37.606 --> 00:02:40.439
(uplifting music)

00:02:41.520 --> 00:02:43.380
Our first trip into Niihau Kauai

00:02:43.380 --> 00:02:47.500
was to return ancestral
remains or K..puna iwi,

00:02:47.500 --> 00:02:49.015
and, ever since that trip,

00:02:49.015 --> 00:02:54.015
we have been going back to take
care this old village site.

00:02:57.620 --> 00:03:00.950
And we have also planted
Wauke there to make Kapa.

00:03:01.810 --> 00:03:04.140
And I am still trying to
figure out my K..puna,

00:03:04.140 --> 00:03:07.553
how my ancestors made as
beautifully as they have.

00:03:10.210 --> 00:03:11.500
It's a process.

00:03:11.500 --> 00:03:13.633
We have to relearn these things.

00:03:15.349 --> 00:03:16.550
- Voila

00:03:16.550 --> 00:03:18.390
- [Narrator] So it's a place of old Hawaii

00:03:18.390 --> 00:03:21.290
that brings much to our understanding

00:03:21.290 --> 00:03:23.463
of perhaps how life used to be.

00:03:29.110 --> 00:03:31.470
- Come in right here and you
gotta cut it at the bottom.

00:03:31.470 --> 00:03:33.690
- [Narrator] I like to
teach my children about Kapa

00:03:33.690 --> 00:03:35.130
because first of all they have to learn

00:03:35.130 --> 00:03:36.348
how to take care of the plants

00:03:36.348 --> 00:03:38.780
and then they have to
learn how to harvest them

00:03:38.780 --> 00:03:39.643
and how to clean them

00:03:39.643 --> 00:03:41.452
and how to pound them.

00:03:41.452 --> 00:03:44.285
(pounding sounds)

00:03:46.350 --> 00:03:47.600
They know who they are.

00:03:47.600 --> 00:03:49.000
They know where they live.

00:03:49.000 --> 00:03:50.110
They know why they live here

00:03:50.110 --> 00:03:52.110
and what they can do to help.

00:03:52.110 --> 00:03:54.400
How do Hawaiians see the land,

00:03:54.400 --> 00:03:59.400
that the land is the chief and
that people are its servants.

00:03:59.677 --> 00:04:02.594
(reflective music)

